<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="money-and-immediate-help"><span aria-hidden="true">01</span>Protect money and get independent help</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If you transferred money, shared card details or approved a payment, contact the bank or card provider immediately through an official channel. Ask what protective action is available, such as stopping or reviewing a transaction. The provider decides what can be done; no outcome is guaranteed. See <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://www.scamwatch.gov.au/stop-check-protect/what-to-do-if-youve-been-scammed">Scamwatch’s advice on what to do after a scam</a>.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Do not use a phone number, link or reply address supplied by the suspicious contact. If the situation involves cybercrime, you can report it through <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://www.cyber.gov.au/report-and-recover/report">ReportCyber</a>. Scamwatch also accepts scam reports. If there is an immediate threat to life or risk of harm, call 000. These reporting pathways are not a promise that an individual loss will be recovered.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="account-and-device"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Match the response to what happened</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A suspicious message or clicked link may be an exposure to assess, not proof that an account or device was compromised. Urgency increases if you entered a password, payment detail or verification code; installed software; approved a sign-in; or gave someone remote access.</p><ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-muted"><li><strong>Clicked only:</strong> stop interacting, close the page and avoid reopening the link to investigate.</li><li><strong>Credentials shared:</strong> use a trusted device and the provider’s official recovery or security process.</li><li><strong>Payment information shared:</strong> contact the bank or card provider immediately.</li><li><strong>Remote access or software installed:</strong> end the session if safe, contact the bank if financial information was accessible, and seek appropriate device support.</li></ul><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">For remote-access scams, <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://www.cyber.gov.au/about-us/advisories/protect-yourself-remote-access-scams">Cyber.gov.au advises ending suspicious calls and contacting the organisation independently</a>. Removing a tool alone is not proof that a device is clean.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="verify-unexpected-contact"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Verify an unexpected support contact before sharing access</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If someone contacts you unexpectedly claiming to be from a bank, phone company, software provider or government service, pause before sharing your screen, installing software, reading out a code or approving a sign-in. A familiar logo, caller ID or a detail about you does not prove who is calling.</p><ol class="mb-6 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-muted"><li>End the unexpected contact politely. Do not use its phone number, link or reply address to verify it.</li><li>Find the organisation’s contact details independently from an official app, card, statement or website you type yourself.</li><li>Ask the organisation through that independent channel whether it contacted you and what action, if any, is actually required.</li><li>If you already shared access, a password, payment detail or code, stop the conversation and follow the incident path in this guide.</li></ol><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Legitimate support can wait while you verify it independently. Do not let a caller’s urgency turn a routine support question into remote access or account recovery performed for you.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="trusted-contact-and-next-steps"><span aria-hidden="true">04</span>Bring in a trusted person</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Ask someone you trust to sit with you while you check official contact details, review what was shared and write down decisions. A second person can help slow down pressure, especially when a caller demands secrecy, urgency or another payment.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If you need specialist support after a cyber incident, <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://www.scamwatch.gov.au/stop-check-protect/what-to-do-if-youve-been-scammed">Scamwatch identifies IDCARE as a support option</a>. Use official websites and phone numbers rather than details supplied by the caller. Keep the support focused on practical next steps, not on proving a theory about the scammer.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="record-and-report"><span aria-hidden="true">05</span>Record, report and watch for follow-up scams</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">After urgent money and account actions, make a private timeline. Note when contact occurred, what you observed, what you shared or installed in broad categories, which provider you contacted and any report or case reference. Keep original messages where safe, but do not reopen suspicious links or continue the conversation.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Be cautious of anyone who contacts you later promising guaranteed recovery, refunds or special access for an upfront fee. A follow-up offer can be another scam. Recheck important accounts through their official security pages and tell your trusted contact if the situation changes.</p>
